# Client-side Penca env template.
#
# These are the environment variables `PencaClient` reads at runtime.
# There is nothing server-internal in this file — just the 3 gRPC channel
# URLs the client opens (one per microservice) plus the Flight SQL URL.
#
# Flow:
#   1. `just penca-up` starts the infra + servicer containers using
#      `docker/{test,dev}.env` for host-port bindings.
#   2. After containers are healthy, the Justfile queries the actual host
#      ports (`docker compose port <service> <internal-port>`) and sed-replaces
#      the __*_PORT__ placeholders below. The result is written to
#      `docker/.client.env` (gitignored).
#   3. Test recipes source `.client.env` before running pytest, so
#      `ClientSettings` picks up the
#      right URLs.
#
# Each worktree generates its own `.client.env` with its own random ports,
# so parallel worktrees don't conflict.
#
# Server-internal env vars (DATABASE_URL, OBJECT_STORAGE_*, per-service knobs)
# live inline in `docker/compose.yml` — they never leave the
# container network, so they do not belong in this file.
#
# Integration tests also source `docker/.baseline.env` (from template.baseline.env)
# to open a direct Postgres connection for white-box assertions about
# storage state the gRPC API does not expose.
PENCA_QUERY_URL=localhost:__QUERY_PORT__
PENCA_WRITE_URL=localhost:__WRITE_PORT__
PENCA_LIFECYCLE_URL=localhost:__LIFECYCLE_PORT__
PENCA_SQL_URL=localhost:__PENCA_SQL_PORT__
